      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Named after Queen Isabel II, the town of Isabel Segunda was founded in 1844 by Governor Francisco Saínz.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.viequestravel.com/about-vieques/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Vieques Bioluminescent Bay, also called Mosquito Bay, earned Guinness World Records as the brightest bay in 2006 and is a national landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vieques%2C_Puerto_Rico",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Fortín Conde de Mirasol, a mid‑19th‑century Spanish fort, now houses the Vieques Museum of Anthropology, History and Art after a 1990s restoration.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.viequestravel.com/about-vieques/history",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Vieques sits at a natural crossroads between the Atlantic and Caribbean, where currents, reefs, and shallow shelves shaped navigation and settlement.",
